  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">TZ JENGA_I&apos;M POWERFUL</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Gender-based violence (GBV) is a serious issue in the lives of many women and disabled persons in Tanzania. It stems from religiousbeliefs, social practices, and laws that favor men over women. Gender discrimination and gender fairness are increasingly being recognized in Tanzania. This awakening includes an increasing recognition of the prevalence of gender-based violence and the methods and extent to which it impacts not only women and girls, but also men and boys. Despite the fact that Tanzanian law has made strides in preventing and sentencing GBV offences. For example, the Sexual Offence Special Provisions Act of 1998 imposes heavy punishments on perpetrators of sexual violence. However, inequities persist in religious violence, feminism exclusion,and the judicial system. Domestic abuse, in particular, is only briefly and ambiguously handled.

Religion and feminism are frequently considered as impediments to gender equality. Stories of gender-based violence committed in the name of religion and feminism&apos;s advocacy, which sometimes exclude males and make progress toward gender equality and the abolition of gender-based violence difficult. As a result, in many cases, the concerns of faith and feminism toward gender parity are dismissed as too hardto address. However, there is a way to untangle this through complicated multi-institution collaboration by church leaders and feminists. To address the issue of gender-based violence in the name of religion, the idea proposes bringing together faith leaders to speak about religious freedom and respect for a diversity of religious beliefs and practices, allowing minority groups, including women, to be at the table and have their vital, productive, and creative voices heard. In our setting, faith leaders understand the significance of women to our economy; thus, gender-based discrimination in the name of religion continues to impact women&apos;s productivity in the economy. The plan also includes organizing a two-day gender equality seminar for religion leaders and feminism, as well as introducing the concept of a collaborative and inclusive approach to gender parity. Formation of play (Drama) groups with scripts depicting the roles of church leaders and feminism on gender-based violence. Formation of an art group (drummer and dancers), photos with messages on how women&apos;s rights are infringed,and how to avoid exploitation and oppression of women. The art group will be provided with supplies to manufacture arts materials for cash generation in order to maintain the art group sustainable once the project is over.

The long-term impact of this idea is based on the fact that the formulated art hub for drummers, dancers, and photographers who will be exhibiting their work continuously towards advocacy on gender-based violence, as well as these women with representation of 80% and 20% of male will be producing art accessories as part of income generating activity, dancers and drummers will be organizing events from time to time to showcase the issue of gender-basedviolence but also as parity.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="24">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">JENGA KILIMANJARO OUTREACH</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Jenga Kilimanjaro Outreach (is a non-governmental organization focused on youth and women, headquartered in Mwanga Kilimanjaro, andregistered in Tanzania on August 18th, 2015 under NGO Act 2002 registration number 00 NGO/ 00008132. JENGA supplements government efforts by assisting in the implementation of numerous policies, bringing development to citizens. Our primary focus is on youth and women. We strive to improve economic independence, eliminate all forms of prejudice, improve food security, and safeguard natural resources through community-based actions.

Vision: To be a regional force and a partner in a movement dedicated to eradicating poverty and social injustice by empowering women and youth through capacity building and economic growth.

Mission: To stimulate and encourage the appropriate use of community resources for the self-sufficiency of marginalized women and youth in rural areas.

The charity has assisted 50 pregnant women and breast-feeding mothers. Through our WOMEN RISE UP CHARITY program, pregnant women were provideddelivery equipment, and breast-feeding mothers were given gifts for their children such as pampers, shirts, and oils. &#xf0a1; The group has also reached out to 35 women in Bagamoyo by mentoring them on entrepreneurshipskills and providing them with land for small businesses like as selling vegetables and fruits through our JENGA FOR HER MALKIA initiative.

Jenga&apos;s goals include, but are not limitedto;

To educate the rural population about VSLAs (Village Saving and Loan Associations) and connect them with formal financial service providers.

To enable women to participate in family, community, regional, and national decision-making.

Promoting the health and well-being of the community&apos;s members and working together as neighbors regardless of age, ethnic origin, ability, sex, belief, or political affiliation, while acknowledging the value of our many diversity.

To carry out and promote both environmental improvement and practical conservation, as well as toeducate, encourage, and support the local populace in environmental issues through collaboration with statutory and non-statutory organizations.</narrative>
  </participating-org>
